title: "What does the reconstructed airframe look like in three views?"
subtitle: "The reconstruction was checked only against two pose-fitted photographs. Nobody has yet seen the shape itself drawn straight, and every later chapter forks this geometry."
---




**Answer.** The orthographic drawing (@fig-three-view) reveals the reconstructed F-16 airframe in side elevation ($X$–$Z$), front elevation ($-Y$–$Z$), plan view ($X$–$Y$), and isometric projection. Unlike the oblique photographic poses evaluated during the [initial reconstruction](2026-10-07-01-can-we-reconstruct-the-ft-f-16-viper-within-tolerance.qmd), the orthogonal views clearly display the compound delta geometry: leading-edge root strakes swept at [72\.3°]{.key} transition smoothly into outboard wings swept at [36\.8°]{.key} spanning [914 mm]{.key}. The profile captures key Viper features, including the underslung chin intake cowl, bubble canopy, dorsal spine, single vertical fin with fillet ([210 mm]{.key} tall), twin canted ventral strakes, and all-moving stabilators ([525 mm]{.key} span) set at anhedral.

::: {#cell-fig-three-view .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

airplane.draw_three_view(show=False)
plt.show()
```

